# head — real-world examples

## First 10 lines of a file (default)

```sh
head file
```

## First N lines

```sh
head -n N file
```

## First N bytes

```sh
head -c N file
```

## Everything EXCEPT the last 5 lines (GNU head)

```sh
head -n -5 file
```

## Peek at several config files at once, with headers

```sh
head -n 5 /etc/zypp/repos.d/*.repo
```

## Top of a pipe: heaviest processes, biggest files

```sh
ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -6
du -sh /srv/plex/* | sort -rh | head -10
```

## Generate a quick random token

```sh
head -c 16 /dev/urandom | xxd -p
```

| Flag | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| -n N | first N lines (-n -N: all but last N) |
| -c N | first N bytes |
| -q | never print filename headers |
| -v | always print filename headers |
